# otpme.schema -- OTPme-specific LDAP attribute and object class
# definitions.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# OID assignment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The base arc used below is the Private Enterprise Number assigned to
# OTPme by IANA (https://pen.iana.org/):
#
#   1.3.6.1.4.1.66639
#
# Sub-arc layout (kept stable across future OTPme schema additions):
#   1.3.6.1.4.1.66639.1.<n>   attribute types
#   1.3.6.1.4.1.66639.2.<n>   object classes
#
# Never reuse a sub-OID for a different attribute or object class. Once
# an OID has been published a directory may still hold entries created
# with it, and a changed meaning breaks their schema check. Retire the
# definition and take the next free number instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

#-----------------------------------------------------------
# Attribute types
#-----------------------------------------------------------

# Private / recovery e-mail address, distinct from the corporate ``mail``
# attribute inherited from inetOrgPerson. Used by the SSO portal password
# reset flow to send a recovery token to an address the user controls
# outside the organisation.
#
# Syntax is Directory String (UTF-8) rather than the traditional IA5
# String used by ``mail``, so internationalised mailboxes (EAI, RFC 6530
# -- e.g. ``mueller@muenchen.de`` with UTF-8 localpart or domain) can be
# stored verbatim without a Punycode/ACE round-trip in the UI layer.
attributetype ( 1.3.6.1.4.1.66639.1.1
	NAME 'otpmeRecoveryMail'
	DESC 'OTPme: private/recovery e-mail address for password reset (EAI-capable)'
	EQUALITY caseIgnoreMatch
	SUBSTR caseIgnoreSubstringsMatch
	SYNTAX 1.3.6.1.4.1.1466.115.121.1.15{256}
	SINGLE-VALUE )

# Additional e-mail address that delivers to the same mailbox as the
# users ``mail`` attribute. Multi-valued: a user usually carries several
# aliases (role addresses, former names, short forms).
#
# OTPme brings its own attribute instead of reusing one of the existing
# candidates: ``nisMailAlias`` is STRUCTURAL, so it cannot be put on a
# user entry that is already an inetOrgPerson, and ``mailLocalAddress``
# (inetLocalMailRecipient) is IA5 only and tied to the sendmail/postfix
# routing semantics of that draft. This one is a plain alias list with
# no routing meaning attached.
#
# Syntax is Directory String (UTF-8) for the same reason as
# otpmeRecoveryMail above: internationalised mailboxes (EAI, RFC 6530)
# can be stored verbatim without a Punycode/ACE round-trip.
#
# Note the matching rule differs from the one of ``mail``
# (caseIgnoreIA5Match), so a mail server that resolves an address over
# both has to filter them separately, e.g.
# ``(|(mail=x@y)(otpmeMailAlias=x@y))``.
attributetype ( 1.3.6.1.4.1.66639.1.2
	NAME 'otpmeMailAlias'
	DESC 'OTPme: additional e-mail address delivering to the users mailbox (EAI-capable)'
	EQUALITY caseIgnoreMatch
	SUBSTR caseIgnoreSubstringsMatch
	SYNTAX 1.3.6.1.4.1.1466.115.121.1.15{256} )

#-----------------------------------------------------------
# Object classes
#-----------------------------------------------------------

# Auxiliary class that hangs OTPme-specific user attributes off any
# existing structural class (typically inetOrgPerson). Kept AUXILIARY so
# it composes freely with the standard person/organizationalPerson/
# inetOrgPerson stack that OTPme users already carry.
objectclass ( 1.3.6.1.4.1.66639.2.1
	NAME 'otpmeUser'
	DESC 'OTPme: auxiliary class for OTPme-specific user attributes'
	SUP top
	AUXILIARY
	MAY ( otpmeRecoveryMail $ otpmeMailAlias ) )
